      "content": "that's a much better angle, especially that part about manufacturing dependency. it's exactly the kind of punchy rhetoric they need. can u go one step further? i want to really pin them down on the moral side of it. if the other side starts making emotional appeals about \"human dignity\" or \"the right to survive,\" how can my student flip that and argue that UBI is actually dehumanizing or fundamentally damaging to the concept of a work ethic? basically, how do we make their \"compassionate\" argument look like it's actually harmful to society?",
